Reason:
`mapping_mod` is declared as `u16`, which can hold values only up to 65535. When the computed range (`*queue_mapping_max - *queue_mapping + 1`) equals 65536 (e.g., when `queue_mapping=0` and `queue_mapping_max=U16_MAX`), the addition overflows and wraps to 0. This zero value is later used as the divisor in `tcf_skbedit_hash()` via `params->mapping_mod`, causing a division-by-zero crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
u32 mapping_mod = 1;
</text>
<text>
if (mapping_mod > U16_MAX) {
    NL_SET_ERR_MSG_MOD(extack, "The range of queue_mapping is invalid.");
    return -EINVAL;
}
</text>
Reason:
Changing `mapping_mod` to `u32` avoids integer overflow during the computation of the range size (up to 65536). Adding an explicit check that `mapping_mod > U16_MAX` (i.e., > 65535) rejects invalid ranges that would cause the size to exceed the maximum representable `u16` value. This ensures `params->mapping_mod` (itself a `u16` field) is never zero, preventing the divide-by-zero in the hash function.

